---
import Icon from "./Icon.astro";
---

<section id="for-developers" class="section section--surface fordev">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="eyebrow"><Icon name="terminal" size={14} /> For Tor Project developers</span>
    <h2>What the Flatpak launcher does for Linux, this does for every desktop.</h2>
    <p class="lede" style="max-width: 68ch;">
      <a href="https://flathub.org/apps/org.torproject.torbrowser-launcher">Tor Browser Launcher</a>
      is the closest thing to this on Linux today: fetch the current release, verify it, install it,
      launch it — distributed as a Flatpak. It's a good tool, but it's Linux-only by construction,
      and it's Python + GTK running inside a Flatpak sandbox. Tor Browser Installer covers the same
      job — fetch, confirm, verify, install, launch — as a single native Rust binary, and the exact
      same source builds it for macOS and Windows too, not only Linux.
    </p>

    <div class="grid grid--3 fordev__grid">
      <div class="card fordev__card">
        <h3>Same job, one codebase</h3>
        <p>Release-JSON fetch, SHA-256 checksum, PGP signature against the Tor Browser
          Developers key, then a platform-native install step — the whole pipeline is one
          <code>install.rs</code>, gated by <code>cfg(target_os = ...)</code> rather than three
          separate platform-specific tools.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card fordev__card">
        <h3>Not limited to Linux, or to Flatpak</h3>
        <p>No sandbox runtime or Flatpak permissions model to reason about. The macOS build
          mounts the <code>.dmg</code> and copies the <code>.app</code> the way Finder would;
          the Windows build drives the NSIS installer's own silent-install flags.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card fordev__card">
        <h3>Nothing hidden</h3>
        <p>Every shell command the app runs is logged to a visible “View commands” panel in
          the UI as it happens — the same transparency principle Tor Browser Launcher's users
          already expect, carried to every platform.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="fordev__note">
      <Icon name="beta" size={16} />
      <p>
        This is an independent, unofficial project, not a Tor Project product, and it hasn't
        been reviewed or endorsed by the Tor Project. It's presented here as a working
        starting point for that conversation, not a finished replacement — see the
        <a href="/security">security page</a> for exactly what it does and doesn't cover
        today, including the plain-HTTPS download and the current beta status.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .fordev__grid { margin-top: 32px; }
  .fordev__card { padding: 22px; }
  .fordev__card h3 { color: var(--app-purple-deep); margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: 1.05rem; }
  .fordev__card p { color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.92rem; margin: 0; }
  .fordev__note {
    display: flex;
    gap: 12px;
    align-items: flex-start;
    margin-top: 32px;
    padding: 16px 18px;
    background: var(--app-purple-soft);
    border: 1px solid var(--app-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    color: var(--app-purple-deep);
  }
  .fordev__note :global(svg) { flex: none; margin-top: 3px; }
  .fordev__note p { margin: 0; font-size: 0.88rem; }
</style>
